 ### Graphical Rule Specification
-A GUI-based method for constructing illustration rules using semantic properties, visual attributes, and logical operators, represented as interactive expression trees [S2]. The interface contains three types of widgets: 1) Semantic data property widgets (top row) containing semantic-property and membership-function images; 2) Expression tree nodes (bottom part) where leaf nodes represent semantic properties and interior nodes represent rules/sub-rules with result images; 3) Visual attribute widget (left) [S2]. Rule creation involves dragging a connection between two semantic-property widgets to specify a rule based on the two properties [S2]. Operator selection uses a design gallery rendering result images for different logical combinations (AND, OR, NOT) of the properties to select the desired operator [S2]. Rule extension is performed by drawing a connection from a semantic-property widget to an expression-tree widget to include the semantic property as an additional operand [S2]. Rule combination is achieved by drawing a connection between two root nodes of different expression trees to combine them into a single rule [S2].
+**Definition:** A GUI-based method for constructing illustration rules using semantic properties, visual attributes, and logical operators, represented as interactive expression trees [S2].
+**Steps:**
+1.  **Rule Creation:** Drag a connection between two semantic-property widgets to specify a rule based on the two properties [S2].
+2.  **Operator Selection:** Use a design gallery rendering result images for different logical combinations (AND, OR, NOT) of the properties to select the desired operator [S2].
+3.  **Rule Extension:** Draw a connection from a semantic-property widget to an expression-tree widget to include the semantic property as an additional operand [S2].
+4.  **Rule Combination:** Draw a connection between two root nodes of different expression trees to combine them into a single rule [S2].
+**Layout:** The UI contains three widget types: 1) Semantic data property widgets (top row) containing semantic-property and membership-function images; 2) Expression tree nodes (bottom part) where leaf nodes represent semantic properties and interior nodes represent rules/sub-rules with result images; 3) Visual attribute widget (left) [S2].
 
 ### Case-Based Reasoning Transfer Function Design
-A transfer function design workflow where users select Sources of Interest (SOIs) directly in visualization space (2D cross-sections or 3D DVR) instead of indirect TF widgets [S3, unofficial]. The user draws a line (ray) in 2D cross-sectional views or clicks a point in 3D DVR to select target SOIs [S3, unofficial]. The system retrieves matching SOIs via a two-stage CBR method [S3, unofficial]. TFs are constructed by combining individual tent-shape components, each representing a retrieved SOI [S3, unofficial]. If false-positive SOIs are retrieved, the user manually eliminates the corresponding TF components [S3, unofficial]. False-negative SOIs (missing structures) require manual addition, but starting from scratch is avoided [S3, unofficial].
-
-### Multi-level Interface for Volume Illustration
-An interface design that abstracts complex rendering parameters into levels of expertise (Novice, Expert) and levels of representation (Schematic, Complex, Realistic) to manage the large number of parameters in volume illustration [S4, unofficial]. The interface is structured into High level (Motifs), Middle level (Parameters), and Low level (Transfer functions/Rendering parameters) [S4, unofficial]. At the Novice Level, high-level parameters and motifs are presented, allowing users to focus on attentive focus and unconscious orientation of structures without being overwhelmed by data complexity [S4, unofficial]. At the Expert Level, transfer functions (low level) and rendering parameters are presented, allowing experts to quickly focus on specific portions of data and relevant structures [S4, unofficial]. Representation levels define motifs with feedback from professional medical illustrators to create schematic/simplistic, complex, or realistic representations [S4, unofficial].
+**Definition:** A transfer function design workflow where users select Sources of Interest (SOIs) directly in visualization space (2D cross-sections or 3D DVR) instead of indirect TF widgets [S3, unofficial].
+**Steps:**
+1.  User draws a line (ray) in 2D cross-sectional views or clicks a point in 3D DVR to select target SOIs [S3, unofficial].
+2.  System retrieves matching SOIs via a two-stage Case-Based Reasoning (CBR) method [S3, unofficial].
+3.  TFs are constructed by combining individual tent-shape components, each representing a retrieved SOI [S3, unofficial].
+4.  User manually eliminates TF components for false-positive SOIs if retrieval errors occur [S3, unofficial].
+**Pitfall:** False-negative SOIs (missing structures) require manual addition, but starting from scratch is avoided [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### Parallel Coordinates Style Interface
-A parallel coordinates style interface that provides an overview of rendering options and transfer function settings, allowing the user to easily explore different parameters and backtrack the visualization history [S1, unofficial]. Tory et al. [89] propose this interface where the user can easily explore different parameters and backtrack the visualization history using the proposed interface [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Topology-controlled Transfer Function UI
-To select a topological zone in a rendered image, a ray is projected through the data from the pixel. The first 'visible' topological zone is selected by evaluating the volume rendering integral for the ray and finding the first sample with opacity above a threshold [S5]. The fully augmented contour tree is used to determine the topological zone to which the selected sample (from ray) or pixel (from slice) belongs [S5]. The contour tree is displayed in two representations: a TOPORRERY view (3D radial layout where height corresponds to function value) and a traditional 2D view (y-coordinate corresponds to function value) [S5]. A vertical transfer function editor is included next to the contour tree interface. The y-coordinates of the contour tree interface and the transfer function editor are linked to provide contextual topological information [S5]. For volume-rendered data with multiple layers, ray casting visibility is not straightforward; a slice view is provided as an alternative where the user selects the topological zone's intersection with that slice [S5].
+**Definition:** A parallel coordinates style interface that provides an overview of rendering options and transfer function settings, allowing the user to easily explore different parameters and backtrack the visualization history [S1, unofficial].
+**Steps:** Tory et al. [89] propose this interface where the user can easily explore different parameters and backtrack the visualization history using the proposed interface [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### WYSIWYG Semantic Representation
-An adaptation of the WYSIWYG (What You See Is What You Get) concept to volumetric exploration, implementing a high-level semantic representation with drawing tools [S6, unofficial].
+**Definition:** An adaptation of the WYSIWYG (What You See Is What You Get) concept to volumetric exploration, implementing a high-level semantic representation with drawing tools [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of a "spreadsheet-like interface" for transfer function design was proposed by Jankun-Kelly and Ma [51], allowing users to explore a range of parameter combinations simultaneously and compare results side-by-side [S1, unofficial]. Tory et al. [89] proposed a parallel coordinates style interface for overviewing rendering options and transfer function settings [S1, unofficial]. Tzeng and Ma [92] proposed using ISODATA clustering on a subset of data voxels to classify the volume, displaying classified features in a cluster-space user interface [S1, unofficial]. Guo, Mao, and Yuan (2011) adapted the WYSIWYG concept to volumetric exploration, implementing a high-level semantic representation with drawing tools [S6, unofficial].
+The concept of spreadsheet-like interfaces for volume rendering was proposed by Jankun-Kelly and Ma [51], allowing users to explore a range of parameter combinations simultaneously and compare results side-by-side [S1, unofficial]. Tory et al. [89] introduced a parallel coordinates style interface to provide an overview of rendering options and transfer function settings, facilitating parameter exploration and history backtracking [S1, unofficial]. Guo, Mao, and Yuan (2011) adapted the WYSIWYG concept to volumetric exploration, implementing a high-level semantic representation with drawing tools [S4, unofficial]. More recently, CBR-based approaches have enabled users to select SOIs directly in visualization space, reducing the cognitive load of indirect TF widget manipulation [S3, unofficial].
 
